From 40ff9caaa977b90b00fda239bb8c5aeb42e1b644 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ServerOmega <69215510+ServerOmega@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 04:28:08 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Docs: structured events, match id, timeline tool and
 measured cost.

Describes the events each component writes, how the card server
rebuilds the matchmaker's match id without changing any reply, gap
limits, privacy rules, config, and the load-test numbers with logging
on and off.

Co-authored-by: Grok Bot <grok-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 ONLINE_MATCHMAKING.md | 78 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 78 insertions(+)

diff --git a/ONLINE_MATCHMAKING.md b/ONLINE_MATCHMAKING.md
index e14c689..1acb8d5 100644
--- a/ONLINE_MATCHMAKING.md
+++ b/ONLINE_MATCHMAKING.md
@@ -700,6 +700,84 @@ Both are untested on a cabinet. The cabinet's own formula has not been reverse e
   PublicMatchmaker build: `/status` 200 from a non-LAN source address, `/status/x`, `/`, `/ui` 403, 429 after the
   burst; a 16-cabinet fake load test showed searching / in-match moving as rooms formed.
 
+## Structured events (2026-11-17, branch `structured-logging`): unit and local load tests only, not yet tried on cabinets
+
+The card server and the matchmaker also write one JSON line per match step to `Logs/events/events-YYYYMMDD.jsonl`. The text logs (`Logs/log-*.txt`) don't change. The launcher's Health tab, presence and match timeline read the events first and fall back to the text rules. The full schema, shared with the clean-room card server, is in exvs2-card-server `docs/EVENTS.md`.
+
+```json
+{"v":1,"ts":"2026-11-17T03:59:16.624-08:00","src":"mm","ev":"match_found","match":"m-tonamp-1","room":1,"cab":"c4975ea73","node":1000,"apply":27994062,"world":"mix-match","wait_s":0.2,"fetch_s":2.7}
+{"v":1,"ts":"2026-11-17T02:00:45.000-08:00","src":"card","ev":"gap_warning","cab":"c9fbd99da","apply":9,"stage":"loading","waited_s":20.5,"limit_s":20,"after":"peer_connect"}
+{"v":1,"ts":"2026-11-17T04:00:57.354-08:00","src":"mm","ev":"disconnect","match":"m-tonamp-49","room":49,"cab":"cf973b159","node":1408,"reason":"cancel-after-match","since_s":3}
+```
+
+* **Events.**
+  * Matchmaker: `queue_start` and `queue_stop`; `room_created`, one per room, with the cab keys; `match_found` and `load_start` when the cab fetches its room; `room_ended`; and `disconnect` when a room ends with cancel-after-match or timeout.
+  * Card server:
+    * `card_insert` on LoadCard.
+    * `peer_connect` on CheckCommunication, with the UDP result, RTT, NAT type and search seconds.
+    * `load_end` and `battle_start` on SaveBattleLogOn.
+    * `result_saved` on SaveVsmOnResult or SaveVsmResult.
+    * `disconnect` on a hole-punch failure, or on SaveFailedBattleLogOn with `my_fault`.
+  * Both: `gap_warning`, and `log_dropped` if the queue ever overflows.
+  * 21-01 is never sent to a server, so `disconnect` has a `code` only when one is known.
+* **One match ID: `m-<boot>-<room>`.**
+  * The matchmaker makes a boot ID when it starts (base 36 of its start time) and numbers its rooms.
+  * The cabinet repeats the room ID in its result and failed-battle requests, so the card server rebuilds the same match ID.
+  * The card server gets the boot ID from the new optional `"boot"` field in the matchmaker's `GET /status`. It reads it once a minute in the background, never during a request. With the in-process matchmaker it reads the boot ID directly.
+  * No matchmaking or card reply changes, because the game reads those bytes.
+  * With an older matchmaker that has no `boot` field, card events carry only `room`. The timeline then joins on room + cab + time, or on cab + apply ID.
+* **Timeline.**
+  * Each stage is timed per cab. A `gap_warning` is logged once per stage when a stage passes its limit:
+
+    | Stage | Limit |
+    |---|---|
+    | Room fetch | 10 s |
+    | Room to peer connect | 40 s |
+    | Loading (peer connect to battle start) | 20 s |
+    | Battle | 480 s |
+    | Results to next request | 60 s |
+
+  * On real cabinets, room to CheckCommunication took 19–23 s. CheckCommunication to battle log took 14–19 s with two humans, and 30 s once with four.
+  * `tools/match-timeline` rebuilds a match from any events files or folders:
+    * List matches: `dotnet run --project tools/match-timeline -- --list Logs/events`
+    * Show one match: `dotnet run --project tools/match-timeline -- --match m-tonamp-1 Logs/events /copy/of/vps/events`
+    * Change limits with `--fetch`, `--connect`, `--loading` and `--battle`.
+  * The launcher has the same view under Health → **Match timeline…**.
+* **Privacy.** Tests push fake secrets through every event to enforce this.
+  * Events never contain card IDs, chip IDs, access codes, session IDs, pilot or am IDs, tag secrets, serials or IPs.
+  * A cabinet is `cab` = `c` + 8 hex characters of SHA-256("exvs2-ev1|cab|" + serial). It is the same on every server and in the launcher.
+  * An IP appears only as `ip` = `i` + 8 hex characters of an HMAC-SHA-256 with a random per-install salt (`Logs/events/.salt`).
+  * Forbidden keys are dropped. Free text goes through the text-log masking, IPv4 addresses become hashes, and values are cut at 300 characters.
+* **Cost.**
+  * A request only stamps a time and adds the event to the queue, about 0.6 µs.
+  * Masking, JSON (about 4 µs per event) and disk writes run on one background writer thread.
+  * The queue holds 20000 events. When it is full, events are dropped and counted, and requests never wait.
+* **Size.**
+  * One file per day, plus a `-002` part when a file passes a quarter of the cap.
+  * Files older than 14 days are deleted first, then the oldest files above 200 MB total. This runs at start and as files grow.
+* **Config** is `CardServerConfig.StructuredLog`. The section may be missing; these are the defaults:
+  * `Enabled` true. false means no file and no per-request work.
+  * `Directory` `./Logs/events`.
+  * `RetentionDays` 14.
+  * `MaxTotalMegabytes` 200.
+  * `QueueCapacity` 20000.
+  * `FetchMatchmakerBootId` true.
+  * `Gaps`: `RoomFetchSeconds` 10, `ConnectSeconds` 40, `LoadingSeconds` 20, `BattleSeconds` 480, `ResultsSeconds` 60. 0 turns that check off.
+* **Remote matchmaker.** A card server that points at a VPS matchmaker never sees queue events, so its own file starts at `peer_connect`, about 20 s after the match. Copy the VPS `Logs/events` next to it to get the full timeline.
+* **Measured.** 100-person load test on `/match` on a Linux box (240 s plus arrivals, 4 runs on and 4 off; rounds 1–2 used an earlier build that serialized events on the request thread). Client-side latency over all requests:
+
+  | Run | p50 on / off (ms) | p99 on / off (ms) | Server CPU mean on / off | Events | Text log |
+  |---|---|---|---|---|---|
+  | 1 | 1.51 / 1.24 | 5.01 / 4.81 | 2.3 / 1.9 % | 207 KB, 85 rooms | 2.5 MB |
+  | 2 | 1.28 / 1.33 | 4.93 / 3.98 | 2.0 / 2.0 % | 193 KB, 77 rooms | 2.4 MB |
+  | 5 | 1.46 / 1.50 | 4.77 / 5.05 | 2.3 / 2.3 % | 209 KB, 84 rooms | 2.6 MB |
+  | 6 | 1.57 / 1.55 | 9.29 / 6.50 | 3.2 / 2.6 % | 213 KB, 86 rooms | 2.5 MB |
+
+  * The on/off difference is within run-to-run noise. Mean p50 was 1.46 ms on and 1.41 ms off, and p99 went both ways. RSS was the same (about 255 MB).
+  * The card-side micro benchmark (`SL_BENCH=1 dotnet test Tests/Matchmaking.Tests --filter StructuredEventBench`) measured 0.6 µs per request on the request thread.
+  * Size: about 182 bytes per line and about 2.5 KB per room on the matchmaker (queue churn included). On the card server, 5 events and about 0.8 KB per cab per match. That is about 8 % of the text log for the same run.
+  * Per day: the 100-cab non-stop load pace is about 18,500 rooms and about 46 MB a day, so the 200 MB cap would keep about 4 days. A realistic LA day of a few hundred rooms is under 1 MB a day; 4 local cabs playing about 60 matches each is about 0.2 MB.
+
 ## Known gaps / risks
 * CheckMatching / CancelMatching are not authenticated: anyone who knows a node id can cancel that entry.
   (`BindMatchNodesToSourceIp` limits Check/Cancel to the IP that entered, but is not a login.)
